p='content/security/01-fundamentals/secf-kill-chain.md';q=p.replace('.md','.questions.json');at='https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/enterprise/';lm='https://www.lockheedmartin.com/content/dam/lockheed-martin/rms/documents/cyber/LM-White-Paper-Intel-Driven-Defense.pdf';uk='https://www.unifiedkillchain.com/assets/The-Unified-Kill-Chain-Thesis.pdf';pop='https://rvasec.com/slides/2014/Bianco_Pyramid%20of%20Pain.pdf';v='https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1078/';ps='https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001/';mt='https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/m-trends-2025'
def e(x,y,why,src=[uk]):edit(p,x,y,why,src)
def c(i,key,value,why,src=[uk],option=None):qe(q,'secf-kill-chain-q'+str(i),key,value,why,src,option)
e('Breaches are rarely a single clever exploit. They are a sequence:','Many intrusions involve several activities:','Avoidunsupported prevalence.')
e('Each step takes time and leaves traces.','Activities may leave evidence when the relevant telemetry is available.','Notguaranteedvisibletrace.')
e("If an attack is a chain, you don't have to stop every link; breaking any one stops the attack, and seeing any one gives you a chance to respond.","Interrupting a necessary step can stop a particular attack path. Attackers may skip stages or switch paths, so one blocked step does not guarantee the whole campaign has stopped. Observing activity provides an opportunity to investigate and respond.",'Killchainnotuniversallynecessarylinear.')
e('Defensive leverage starts at delivery.','Some reconnaissance can be detected through scanning or web telemetry; delivery is not the first possible defensive intervention.','Originalpaperhasreconcontrols.',[lm])
e('- **Tactics** are the *why*: the attacker\'s short-term goal. The Enterprise matrix has 14.','- **Tactics** are the *why*: the attacker\'s goal for an activity. The live Enterprise tactics page consulted for this review lists 15; counts and names are version-dependent.','Updateprimarycurrentmatrix.',[at])
e('- Each technique lists known groups that use it, **detections** and **mitigations**.','- Technique entries can link procedure examples, mitigations and detection guidance. Available coverage varies; a listed technique does not imply complete detection.','Notallentriescomplete.',[v,at])
e('The 14 Enterprise tactics, roughly in the order an intrusion progresses:','The consulted Enterprise list below is a taxonomy, not a required chronological sequence:', 'Notlinearsequence.',[at])
e('Defense Evasion\nCredential Access','Stealth\nDefense Impairment\nCredential Access','Currenttacticnames.',[at])
e('Attackers increasingly **live off the land**:','Attackers can **live off the land**:','Unsupportedtrend.',[ps])
e('Nothing malicious is installed, so signature-based antivirus has nothing to match.','A malicious binary need not be installed. File-hash matching alone may miss the abuse, but antivirus can also inspect scripts, command content or behaviour; product labels do not determine detection capability.','AVnotsolelyfilehash.',[ps])
e('A foothold as a normal user is rarely enough.','A foothold may not provide the access required for the objective.','Someusersalreadytargets.',[v])
e('and above all **credentials** left lying around','and exposed **credentials**','Unsupportedranking.',[v])
e('Modern ransomware groups usually steal data first and threaten to publish it (**double extortion**),','Some ransomware operations steal data and threaten publication (**double extortion**),','Avoidunverifiedfrequency.',[mt])
e('> [!example] A typical ransomware timeline','> [!example] An illustrative ransomware path','Notempiricaltypical.')
e('> 5. Backups deleted, then ransomware deployed everywhere at once, often overnight or at a weekend.','> 5. Reachable backups are targeted, followed by encryption on systems the attacker can control.','Nouniversalallfleet/timing.')
e('> Each step is a detection opportunity. The final encryption is the *last* and loudest step, not the first.','> These are possible detection opportunities, not an obligatory sequence. Encryption can start earlier, and visibility depends on telemetry.','Notrequiredtimeline.')
e("**Dwell time** is how long an attacker is inside before detection. Mandiant's annual M-Trends reports put the global median at around 10 days in recent years, down from over 400 days in 2011. Ransomware cases tend to be detected fastest, because the attacker announces themselves.","**Dwell time** measures the interval between initial compromise and detection, subject to the evidence available. Mandiant's M-Trends 2025 reports a global median of **11 days for its 2024 investigations**, compared with 10 days for 2023. This is a historical incident-response sample, not a live estimate for every organisation or a safe log-retention target. The source reports five days for adversary-notified cases in that sample.",'Pinmeasurementcohortnotlatestvague.',[mt])
e('Detecting at delivery costs a deleted email. Detecting at impact costs a rebuild.','Stopping a malicious delivery can avoid compromise, while detection after destructive impact may require extensive recovery. Actual response cost is not a fixed function of stage.','Nouniversalrecoverycost.')
e('Blocking a file hash is easy for you and trivial for the attacker to evade: change one byte and recompile. Blocking an IP address costs them a new server. Detecting a **technique** (for example, credential dumping from memory, whatever tool does it) forces them to change how they operate, which is expensive.','Changing a file generally changes its cryptographic hash, so exact-hash blocking can be evaded by a functional variant. An IP change need not require a new server. Robust behavioural detection can impose more adaptation effort, but the Pyramid of Pain is a heuristic: effectiveness depends on telemetry, rule coverage and attacker options.','Notzero-costeveryvariant noruniversalbehaviorguarantee.',[pop])
e('1. **Perimeter-only thinking.** If the only control is at delivery, one phished user means total compromise. Assume breach and invest in later stages.','1. **Perimeter-only thinking.** An entry-control failure can expose later weaknesses. Limit subsequent access and invest in detection beyond initial delivery.','Notallentrytotalcompromise.')
e('2. **Chasing IoCs.** Hash and IP blocklists age in days. Behavioural detections last.','2. **Chasing IoCs.** Indicator and behavioural rules both need validation and maintenance; neither has a fixed useful lifetime.','Unsupportedexpiry.',[pop])
e('- MITRE ATT&CK catalogues 14 Enterprise tactics and many techniques, giving shared vocabulary and a way to measure detection coverage.','- ATT&CK provides versioned tactics and techniques; the consulted Enterprise page lists 15. Test detection coverage instead of inferring it from labels.','Currenttaxonomy.',[at])
e('- Living off the land and valid accounts defeat signature-based defences; detect behaviour instead.','- Legitimate-tool and account abuse can evade file-hash checks; combine identity, content and behavioural evidence.','AVnotallblind.',[ps,v])
e('- The Pyramid of Pain: detecting techniques hurts attackers far more than blocking hashes or IPs.','- The Pyramid of Pain motivates behaviour-focused detection, but adaptation cost and detection success require evidence.','Heuristicnotguarantee.',[pop])
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Evidence limits\n> Timelines and detection examples are illustrative, not executed intrusion exercises. No production SIEM, EDR or backup system was tested. Dwell-time figures are tied to the named historical report; they are not current universal benchmarks.\n\n## Further reading','Explicitexecution/samplinggap.',[mt])
e('- [MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise tactics](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/enterprise/)','- [MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise tactics](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/enterprise/)\n- [Mandiant M-Trends 2025: 2024 investigation results](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/m-trends-2025)\n- [David Bianco: Pyramid of Pain presentation](https://rvasec.com/slides/2014/Bianco_Pyramid%20of%20Pain.pdf)','Fetchedprimarylinks.',[at,mt,pop])
c(1,'explanation','C2 is stage six in the original model, after installation. Real intrusions need not perform every stage in that order.','Scope originalmodel.',[lm,uk],1)
c(2,'workedExample','Tactics describe goals; techniques describe ways of achieving them. The live Enterprise list consulted here has 15 tactics, but the taxonomy changes across versions. One technique can serve more than one tactic, so columns are not a required chronological path.','Currentcount andnonlinearity.',[at])
c(3,'explanation','Correct as a general aim: stopping a delivery before compromise can avoid later harm. Actual investigation cost and damage vary; detection alone does not stop an attack.','Notmonotonicguarantee.',option=3)
c(3,'workedExample','An intercepted malicious attachment can be investigated before execution. An established compromise may require scoping, containment and recovery, and destructive impact can add restoration work. Earlier effective intervention can reduce harm; no fixed cost or obligatory progression follows from the stage label.','Avoidrigidcostsequence.')
c(4,'explanation','Correct. Rebuilding a tool can change its hash while retaining its function. Behavioural rules can be more resilient to such changes, but their own coverage must be tested.','Notallbytes preservefunction noruniversalexpense.',[pop],0)
c(4,'workedExample','The pyramid orders hashes, IPs, domains, host/network artefacts, tools and TTPs as a heuristic for adaptation effort. The recompiled sample evades a rule matching only its old hash. Combine precise indicators with validated behaviour detections; neither is automatically permanent or complete.','Heuristicnotzerocost.',[pop])
c(5,'text','Only comparing executable-file hashes against a known-malware hash list','Distinguishfilehash-onlyfromallAV.',[ps],0)
c(5,'explanation','The scenario uses legitimate executables whose hashes need not be on that list. This does not mean modern antivirus cannot inspect malicious scripts or behaviour.','AvoidallAVblindness.',[ps],0)
c(5,'explanation','Correct. Correct RP/origin-bound authentication resists this credential-phishing path, though passkeys can still involve local prompts and recovery requires protection.','Passkeynotabsenceofprompt.',['https://www.w3.org/TR/webauthn-3/'],1)
c(5,'workedExample','Known-malware executable hashes alone may miss valid-account abuse using existing tools. Phishing-resistant authentication, identity anomaly analysis, network restrictions and behavioural telemetry address other parts of the path. Each depends on deployment and testing; no product category guarantees detection.','Correctscopedclaims.',[v,ps])
c(6,'prompt','A backup NAS grants deletion rights to domain administrators. In this scenario attackers first compromise an ordinary account, then obtain domain-admin credentials and delete the backups. Which step enabled that deletion, and what addresses the exposure?','Domainjoin alonenotprovedeleteauthority.')
c(6,'explanation','Correct for the stated permissions. Domain membership alone does not prove all backups are deletable; review actual rights and retention controls. Independent, properly enforced offline or immutable copies reduce this risk.','Nodomainadminallresourcesguarantee.',option=2)
c(6,'workedExample','The question states that domain-admin credentials grant deletion rights. Escalating to that role therefore enables the deletion in this scenario. Restrict backup administration, separate credentials and preserve independently protected offline or immutable copies, then test restoration. A domain-joined NAS is not automatically configured with the assumed rights.','Explicitmodel.')
c(7,'prompt','What does dwell time measure in an incident-response report?','Removevaguecurrentmedian.',[mt])
c(7,'workedExample','It is the estimated interval from initial compromise to detection. M-Trends 2025 reports an 11-day median across its 2024 investigation sample. A median is not a maximum: plan retention using investigation needs, detection delays and applicable requirements, not a universal ten-day target.','Samplemediannotretentionbound.',[mt])
c(8,'prompt','A team marks 120 techniques green because a SIEM rule exists for each. A controlled exercise successfully runs one relevant test for each, confirms expected telemetry, but triggers only 30 matching rules. What is the most accurate conclusion?','Originaldidnotestablishall120tested.')
c(8,'workedExample','Only 30/120 = 25% of the specified cases triggered their expected rules. Investigate the remaining 90 cases and validate rule assumptions, parsing, thresholds and variants. This is a test-case result, not a claim that only 25% of all real attacks are detected.','Denominatorscope.')
c(9,'explanation','Correct. An insider may already have access and skip an external entry path, though insiders can still perform reconnaissance or exploitation.','Notallinsidersskipeverything.',option=2)
c(10,'explanation','A valid signature supports publisher/key attribution and integrity of the signed bytes; it does not establish that the present invocation is benign.','Signaturesnotprovewhoauthoredhuman.',[ps],0)
c(10,'explanation','Correct as a suspicious hypothesis requiring investigation. The tree alone does not prove phishing, exploitation or C2; correlate content, command details, destination and user activity.','Do nottreatpatternconfirmedincident.',[ps],1)
c(10,'workedExample','Office launching encoded PowerShell with an outbound connection warrants investigation, but does not prove a particular intrusion chain. Preserve the relevant process, script, network and identity evidence; contain according to the response plan and corroborated risk. Credential resets and wider hunting depend on findings. Review appropriate Office child-process and macro controls against business needs.','Avoidautomaticcredentialcompromiseresponse.',[ps])
c(11,'prompt','In a ransomware incident, attackers steal sensitive data before encrypting systems. Why are backups alone insufficient for recovery from the incident?','Removeunverifiedfrequency.')
c(11,'explanation','Correct. Restoring data does not undo disclosure. Assess incident-response, communication and applicable notification requirements for the actual data and jurisdiction.','Noautomaticlegalobligations.',option=0)
c(11,'explanation','Restoring systems does not remove stolen copies or establish that the intrusion is eradicated. This was relevant before any particular calendar year too.','Removepre2019misconception.',option=3)
c(11,'workedExample','Encryption can affect integrity and availability. Data theft adds confidentiality harm. Restore operations, investigate and eradicate the intrusion, assess what was disclosed, and use the organisation\'s communication and notification processes. This lesson does not establish jurisdiction-specific legal duties.','Includeintegrity/legalgap.')
